---
import ChartFigure from "../ChartFigure.astro"
import QdotMappingFigure from "./qdot-mapping.figure"
import { BYTES_PER_LANE, LANES, SHUFFLE_MASKS, STEP_BYTES } from "./geometry"

interface Props {
  id?: string
}

const { id = "fig-qdot-mapping" } = Astro.props
---

<ChartFigure
  id={id}
  name="qdot-mapping"
  figure={QdotMappingFigure}
  state={{ mapping: "warp" }}
  title="Two ways to map a matrix-vector product onto a warp"
  desc={`Schematic. With one thread per output, each of a warp's ${LANES} threads walks its own row of weights, so one load touches ${LANES} rows. With one warp per output, the ${LANES} lanes read ${BYTES_PER_LANE} bytes each, ${STEP_BYTES} contiguous bytes of one row per step, then add up their partial sums in ${SHUFFLE_MASKS.length} stages of xor shuffles.`}
  legend={["global", "reg", "cost"]}
  class="fig-tall-readout"
>
  <div class="chart-group" role="group" aria-label="Mapping">
    <button type="button" class="chart-btn" data-group="mapping" data-value="thread" aria-pressed="false">Thread per output</button>
    <button type="button" class="chart-btn" data-group="mapping" data-value="warp" aria-pressed="true">Warp per output</button>
  </div>
  <button type="button" class="chart-btn" data-action="replay">Replay</button>
  <Fragment slot="caption">
    Schematic. The warp form is the Q8_0 decode matvec as phobos-gguf writes it with lanes:{" "}
    <code>for k in range(ctx.lane * 16, K, ctx.lanes * 16)</code>, then <code>sum&lt;over ctx.lane&gt;(acc)</code>,
    and lane 0 stores the output.
  </Fragment>
</ChartFigure>

<style>
  :global(.qm-boundaries) {
    opacity: 0.5;
  }
</style>
